window.docContents[106].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Two-Variable-Canonical-Form":{"contents":"For a second-order equation in two variables, hyperbolicity is encoded by the\nprincipal quadratic form. Its two characteristic directions are the\ncovectors on which that form vanishes. Choosing two independent null covectors\nas a basis of the cotangent space removes the two pure terms from the\nprincipal symbol and leaves one nonzero mixed term. The statement is\npointwise linear algebra.\n\nThe final sum-and-difference change of variables converts that mixed\nderivative into the familiar difference of two pure second derivatives. At\nthe level of principal symbols, this is the algebraic reason a general\ntwo-variable hyperbolic operator resembles the wave operator.\n\n\n\nFor the symmetric principal matrixhyperbolicity means\n. The associated quadratic and polarized\nforms areThe mixed form is the transformed off-diagonal matrix coefficient.\n\nA negative determinant makes the quadratic form  indefinite and\nnondegenerate, so its null cone in the two-dimensional cotangent space is a\npair of distinct lines.\n\nEvery hyperbolic symmetric principal part has real covectors  such\nthat\n\nWhen , the negative determinant makes the characteristic\nquadratic have two distinct real slopes; the corresponding covectors are\nindependent null directions. Polarization shows their mixed pairing cannot\nvanish, since two independent null vectors with zero pairing would make the\nwhole quadratic form zero. When , explicit coordinate covectors\ngive the same conclusions.\n\nExpressing the principal part in a basis of two characteristic covectors sets\nthe transformed pure coefficients  and  to zero, so the\nprincipal expression collapses to , with  the\ntransformed mixed coefficient, that is, the pairing  just\nshown to be nonzero.\n\nLet  and assume  is  near .\nThe chain rule and equality of mixed partials giveMultiplying by the mixed coefficient turns the remaining principal expression\nintoThe nonzero factor  is kept; dividing by it gives the wave\noperator.\n\nSet the new wave coordinates to  and , where\n are the characteristic variables. Expanding both second\nderivatives, the equal mixed partials cancel the cross terms and the surviving\npure terms have opposite signs. Multiplication by the original mixed\ncoefficient gives the displayed factor.\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Transport and Classical Waves","header":"7.2.2. Two-Variable Canonical Form","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Two-Variable-Canonical-Form"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Traveling-Waves-and-Similarity":{"contents":"A traveling wave is the simplest change of independent variables: the single\ncoordinate  follows a profile moving at constant speed , so any\nequation the profile satisfies is an ordinary differential equation in that\ncoordinate. The construction imposes no equation by itself; it is a total\nfunction of the profile and the speed, with time in coordinate  and\nspace in coordinate  of .\n\nThe traveling-wave construction sends a profile  and speed  to\n\nDifferentiating  in  and in  produces the same\n with factors  and , so the traveling ansatz is exactly\nadapted to the transport operator . The\nconclusion is a pointwise identity between totalized partial derivatives on\n.\n\nEvery differentiable traveling-wave profile satisfies the one-dimensional\ntransport equation .\n\nApply the chain rule to . It gives\n and . Therefore\n.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods","header":"9.2.1.4. Traveling Waves and Similarity","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Traveling-Waves-and-Similarity"}});